      "id": 1,
      "text": "Casar was originally called Race Path when it was incorporated in 1890, but a postal mix‑up turned the name into Casar by 1903.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casar%2C_North_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s first school opened in 1889 on a high knoll along Casar‑Lawndale Road, and a two‑story building followed a few years later.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://clevelandcountynchistory.org/casar/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1925, during a wave of school consolidations, a new brick school was built half a mile from the original one‑room building.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://clevelandcountynchistory.org/casar/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Local newspapers first mentioned Casar in 1886, calling it “Casar” in Shelby’s New Era, long before the town’s formal incorporation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
